WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.118
Here's a puzzle.

00:00:02.118 --> 00:00:05.300
Ask Python for 0.1 plus 0.2.

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:07.255
You don't get 0.3.

00:00:07.255 --> 00:00:11.890
You get 0.3, then a long row of zeros, and then a 4.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.360
Is Python broken?

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:15.141
No.

00:00:15.141 --> 00:00:18.984
It comes from how fractions are written in binary.

00:00:18.984 --> 00:00:23.750
To see why, we need to convert numbers between bases, by hand.

00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:26.893
This is Lecture 1, part 3.

00:00:27.243 --> 00:00:34.626
We'll convert numbers from any base to decimal, and then from decimal to binary, whole numbers and fractions.

00:00:34.626 --> 00:00:37.403
And we'll check every answer with Python.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.221
Going to decimal is just the definition of positional notation.

00:00:42.221 --> 00:00:46.880
Write each digit times the weight of its position, then add up the terms.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:50.003
Take 1101.01, in binary.

00:00:50.003 --> 00:00:55.781
Left of the point, the weights are 8, 4, 2, and 1.

00:00:55.781 --> 00:01:00.750
Right of the point, a half, then a quarter.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:06.843
So it's 8, plus 4, plus 1, plus a quarter.

00:01:06.843 --> 00:01:08.620
That's 13.25.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.968
Pause the video and try one yourself.

00:01:12.968 --> 00:01:17.290
Convert 10110.11 from binary to decimal.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.897
The weights are 16, 8, 4, 2, 1, then a half and a quarter.

00:01:23.897 --> 00:01:29.640
16 plus 4 plus 2, plus a half plus a quarter, is 22.75.

00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:34.316
Octal works the same way, with powers of 8.

00:01:34.316 --> 00:01:42.353
24.67 in octal is 2 times 8, plus 4, plus 6 times an eighth, plus 7 times a sixty-fourth.

00:01:42.703 --> 00:01:52.090
That's 16, plus 4, plus 0.75, plus 0.109375.

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.223
20.859375.

00:01:54.573 --> 00:02:00.295
In hex, remember that the letters are digits 10 to 15.

00:02:00.295 --> 00:02:01.460
Take 2AB.C.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:08.453
That's 2 times 256, plus 10 times 16, plus 11, plus 12 sixteenths.

00:02:08.803 --> 00:02:16.948
512, plus 160, plus 11, plus three quarters.

00:02:16.948 --> 00:02:18.243
683.75.

00:02:18.593 --> 00:02:20.733
Any other base works the same way.

00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:23.753
Change the base, and the weights change with it.

00:02:24.533 --> 00:02:26.610
Python can check these.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:34.557
For whole numbers, the `int` function reads a string of digits in any base from 2 to 36.

00:02:34.557 --> 00:02:36.453
1101 in base 2 is 13.

00:02:36.803 --> 00:02:39.147
But `int` doesn't accept a point.

00:02:39.147 --> 00:02:43.196
So the notes write a small function, called `to_decimal`.

00:02:43.196 --> 00:02:45.397
Don't worry about the code yet.

00:02:45.397 --> 00:02:47.883
Lectures 2 to 4 explain every line.

00:02:48.233 --> 00:02:54.070
It walks through the digits from left to right, and multiplies each one by its weight.

00:02:54.070 --> 00:03:00.993
The power starts at the number of digits before the point, minus 1, and goes down by 1 for each digit.

00:03:01.343 --> 00:03:04.503
And all 6 examples from the slides check out.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:08.301
Now the other way: from decimal to binary.

00:03:08.301 --> 00:03:13.660
This takes two methods, one for the integer part, and one for the fraction.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:18.998
For the integer part, divide by 2, and write down the remainder.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:21.803
Then divide the quotient by 2 again.

00:03:21.803 --> 00:03:24.530
Keep going until the quotient is 0.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.426
Here's 57.

00:03:26.426 --> 00:03:30.290
57 is 2 times 28, plus 1.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:34.154
28 is 2 times 14, plus 0.

00:03:34.154 --> 00:03:37.400
Then 14, 7, 3, and 1.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:46.210
Now read the remainders from the bottom up.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:49.555
1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1.

00:03:49.555 --> 00:03:54.670
So 57 is 111001 in binary.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:04:01.310
Check it: 32, plus 16, plus 8, plus 1, is 57.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:06.039
Why is the first remainder the lowest digit?

00:04:06.039 --> 00:04:13.900
57 equals 2 times 28 plus 1 says that 57 is odd, so its last binary digit is 1.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.432
All the other digits together make 2 times 28.

00:04:17.432 --> 00:04:20.544
So the next division finds the next digit up.

00:04:20.544 --> 00:04:24.210
Each division peels off the lowest digit that's left.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:31.228
The most common mistake is reading the remainders from the top down.

00:04:31.228 --> 00:04:34.800
That gives 100111, which is 39, not 57.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.417
The first remainder is the lowest digit.

00:04:38.417 --> 00:04:42.990
And you can always catch the mistake by converting back.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:47.421
The same method works for any base.

00:04:47.421 --> 00:04:51.799
Divide by 8 for octal, or by 16 for hex.

00:04:51.799 --> 00:04:55.630
215 is 327 in octal, and D7 in hex.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:59.277
For a fraction, multiply by 2 instead.

00:04:59.277 --> 00:05:04.272
The integer part of the product, 0 or 1, is the next binary digit.

00:05:04.272 --> 00:05:08.662
Keep only the fractional part, and multiply it by 2 again.

00:05:08.662 --> 00:05:11.160
Stop when the fraction reaches 0.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:16.410
0.875 times 2 is 1.75: digit 1.

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.994
0.75 times 2 is 1.5: digit 1.

00:05:20.994 --> 00:05:25.419
0.5 times 2 is 1.0: digit 1.

00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:27.790
And we're done.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:37.000
This time the digits come out highest first, so read them from the top down.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.914
0.875 is 0.111 in binary.

00:05:39.914 --> 00:05:44.460
A half, plus a quarter, plus an eighth.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.244
0.6875 takes 4 steps.

00:05:48.244 --> 00:05:52.186
The digits are 1, 0, 1, 1.

00:05:52.186 --> 00:05:54.460
So it's 0.1011.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.680
Why does multiplying work?

00:05:56.680 --> 00:06:02.001
Multiplying a binary fraction by 2 shifts every digit 1 place to the left.

00:06:02.001 --> 00:06:07.610
So the first digit after the point jumps in front of it, where we can read it.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.320
For a number with both parts, convert them separately, and join them with the point.

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:15.850
Take 215.6875.

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:20.988
Division turns 215 into 11010111.

00:06:20.988 --> 00:06:28.150
Multiplication turns the fraction into 0.1011.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.493
Put them together.

00:06:30.493 --> 00:06:35.584
Then convert back with our function, to check.

00:06:35.584 --> 00:06:36.580
215.6875.

00:06:37.367 --> 00:06:40.590
But some fractions never stop.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:41.449
Try 0.1.

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:54.767
Multiply by 2, again and again, and the digits 0, 0, 1, 1 repeat forever, just like one third is 0.333, forever, in decimal.

00:06:55.117 --> 00:06:58.411
A computer has to cut it off somewhere.

00:06:58.411 --> 00:07:03.477
So it stores a number very close to 0.1, but not 0.1 itself.

00:07:03.827 --> 00:07:05.669
That's our puzzle.

00:07:05.669 --> 00:07:11.502
The tiny errors in 0.1 and 0.2 show up when you add them.

00:07:11.502 --> 00:07:17.027
And so, asking whether the sum equals 0.3 gives False.

00:07:17.627 --> 00:07:19.648
This isn't a bug in Python.

00:07:19.648 --> 00:07:24.812
Every language that uses binary floating-point numbers does the same.

00:07:24.812 --> 00:07:32.147
Fractions whose denominator is a power of 2, like a half, a quarter, or 0.875, are stored exactly.

00:07:32.934 --> 00:07:34.094
Let's sum up.

00:07:34.444 --> 00:07:38.884
To convert to decimal: digit times weight, then add.

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:44.839
From decimal: divide for the integer part, and read the remainders from the bottom up.

00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:49.074
Multiply for the fraction, and read the digits from the top down.

00:07:49.424 --> 00:07:54.599
Do the two parts separately, then check by converting back.

00:07:54.599 --> 00:07:58.984
And some fractions, like 0.1, never end in binary.

00:07:59.334 --> 00:08:01.147
Here's one to try.

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:03.765
Convert 100.375 to binary.

00:08:03.765 --> 00:08:08.094
Then convert your answer back, to check it.

00:08:08.644 --> 00:08:17.764
In part 4, we'll see a shortcut between binary, octal and hex, and then the units of data: bits, bytes, and memory addresses.

